Note: These are preliminary data and a first look at the eDNA results. Please only share internally with other NOAA & AKBMP partners and collaborators.



# Executive Summary


These are the results of a pilot study of eDNA in Cook Inlet from Nov. 2020 to Nov. 2021. This pilot project consisted of 3 eDNA sample collections: 

1) Monthly samples were collected at two sites in the Kenai River across the year, 

2) Higher frequency sampling of Twentymile River was conducted from August 2021-November 2021, and 

3) Oppurtunistic sampling within and adjacent to Belugas from ADF&G small vessels in August 2021. We successfully designed, optimized, and implemented sample collection and processing methodologies for Beluga specific qPCR assays and vertebrate community metabarcoding assays.


We demonstrate that eDNA metabarcoding can successfully capture changes in fish assemblages across both space and time allowing for comparison to Beluga foraging patterns. Time of sampling across seasons explained the greatest proportion of variance in our fish assemblage data. Site was also signficant, particularly across Cook Inlet, but also within the Kenai River. We observed no statistically signficant difference in depth within the Kenai River.


Ultimately, these results demonstrate that eDNA approaches are an effective tool for capturing changes of Beluga prey assemblages across both space and time. Targeted deployments of eDNA can reveal critical ecological insights into fish ecology and endangered Cook Inlet Beluga foraging patterns.
    
